  var FW_QUESTION_BANK = [
    { q: 'Which of the following best describes an appropriate first stage of a geographical investigation?', options: ['Collecting primary data immediately without any planning', 'Setting an aim or research question and forming a hypothesis', 'Writing the conclusion', 'Presenting the data as a choropleth map'], correct: 1, explain: 'A geographical investigation typically begins with identifying a clear aim or research question and, where appropriate, a testable hypothesis, before planning how data will be collected.', misconception: 'Data collection, presentation and conclusions all come later in the enquiry process — jumping straight to data collection without a clear aim risks collecting the wrong data.', tag: 'Enquiry process' },
    { q: 'A good hypothesis in a geographical investigation should be:', options: ['Impossible to test with the data collected', 'Clear and testable using the data that will be collected', 'A vague general statement', 'The same as the aim'], correct: 1, explain: 'A hypothesis is a testable statement that predicts a relationship, which the investigation’s data collection and analysis can then support or reject.', misconception: 'A hypothesis needs to be specific and testable — a vague statement, or one the data can’t actually test, isn’t useful for a geographical enquiry.', tag: 'Enquiry process' },
    { q: 'Evaluating a geographical investigation mainly involves:', options: ['Repeating the exact same method with no changes', 'Reviewing the reliability of the methods and data, and suggesting realistic improvements', 'Ignoring any limitations in the data', 'Discarding the conclusion entirely'], correct: 1, explain: 'Evaluation involves critically reviewing how reliable the methods and data collected were, and suggesting specific, realistic improvements that could make a repeat investigation more valid.', misconception: 'A good evaluation doesn’t dismiss the whole investigation — it identifies genuine limitations and proposes practical, specific improvements.', tag: 'Enquiry process' },
    { q: 'Random sampling is best described as a method where:', options: ['Every location or person has an equal chance of being selected', 'Only the researcher’s favourite sites are chosen', 'Sites are selected at fixed, regular intervals', 'Only certain groups of people are deliberately excluded'], correct: 0, explain: 'Random sampling gives every possible location or person an equal chance of being selected, often using a random number generator, which helps reduce bias in the sample.', misconception: 'Systematic sampling, not random sampling, selects sites at fixed regular intervals — the two methods work in different ways.', tag: 'Sampling techniques' },
    { q: 'Taking a measurement every 10 metres along a beach is an example of:', options: ['Random sampling', 'Systematic sampling', 'Stratified sampling', 'Opportunistic sampling'], correct: 1, explain: 'Systematic sampling involves collecting data at regular, fixed intervals, such as taking a measurement every 10 metres along a beach transect.', misconception: 'Random sampling would use a random number generator to choose points rather than fixed regular intervals.', tag: 'Sampling techniques' },
    { q: 'Stratified sampling is most useful when:', options: ['A researcher wants to select only the easiest people or sites to reach', 'A population contains distinct sub-groups that need to be proportionally represented, such as different age groups', 'No planning is needed at all', 'Only one type of site exists in the study area'], correct: 1, explain: 'Stratified sampling divides a population into sub-groups (strata), such as age groups, and then samples from each in proportion to its size, ensuring different groups are fairly represented.', misconception: 'Opportunistic sampling, not stratified sampling, relies on selecting whoever or whatever is easiest to reach.', tag: 'Sampling techniques' },
    { q: 'A key limitation of opportunistic sampling is that it:', options: ['Always takes the longest amount of time', 'Can produce biased results, since only readily available people or sites are chosen', 'Always gives every location an equal chance of selection', 'Requires a random number generator'], correct: 1, explain: 'Opportunistic sampling selects whichever people or sites are conveniently available, which is quick, but can introduce bias since the sample may not be representative of the wider population or area.', misconception: 'Opportunistic sampling is usually quicker, not slower, than other methods — its main drawback is the risk of an unrepresentative, biased sample.', tag: 'Sampling techniques' },
    { q: 'Which sampling method uses a random number generator to select specific points or people?', options: ['Systematic sampling', 'Random sampling', 'Opportunistic sampling', 'None of these'], correct: 1, explain: 'Random sampling commonly uses a random number generator, for example to generate grid references or respondent numbers, to ensure an unbiased, equal chance of selection.', misconception: 'Systematic sampling instead relies on a fixed, regular interval rather than random number generation.', tag: 'Sampling techniques' },
    { q: 'A beach profile is constructed mainly to show:', options: ['The chemical composition of the sand', 'The changing height and shape of a beach along a line from the sea to the back of the beach', 'The colour of the pebbles only', 'The temperature of the sea water'], correct: 1, explain: 'A beach profile is measured using equipment such as a clinometer and ranging poles along a transect from the sea to the back of the beach, recording the changing gradient and shape of the beach.', misconception: 'A beach profile records the physical shape and gradient of the beach surface, not chemical composition or water temperature.', tag: 'Coastal primary data' },
    { q: 'The rate and direction of longshore drift can be measured in the field using:', options: ['A wave count only', 'A floating marker (such as an orange), timed as it moves along the beach', 'A random number generator', 'A questionnaire'], correct: 1, explain: 'A common method for measuring longshore drift is timing how far a floating marker, such as an orange, travels along the beach over a set time, to calculate its rate and direction of movement.', misconception: 'A wave count measures how many waves break in a set time, which is a different, though related, piece of coastal fieldwork data.', tag: 'Coastal primary data' },
    { q: 'Measuring pebble size and shape along a beach transect is useful evidence for investigating:', options: ['Local air pollution levels', 'Patterns of erosion, transportation and deposition along the coast', 'Population density in the area', 'Traffic flow near the coast'], correct: 1, explain: 'Pebble size and shape often change systematically along a beach, for example becoming smaller and more rounded due to attrition, providing evidence for patterns of erosion, transportation and deposition.', misconception: 'Pebble data is used to investigate coastal processes, not unrelated variables like traffic or air pollution.', tag: 'Coastal primary data' },
    { q: 'An Environmental Quality Survey (EQS) is a method used to:', options: ['Score and record the visual quality of an environment using a set of criteria, such as litter or noise levels', 'Measure the exact height of a cliff', 'Calculate wave frequency', 'Record only the temperature of the sea'], correct: 0, explain: 'An Environmental Quality Survey uses a scoring system across a range of criteria, such as litter, noise and visual attractiveness, to assess and compare the quality of different locations.', misconception: 'An EQS is a subjective scoring tool for environmental quality, not a method for measuring physical dimensions like cliff height or wave data.', tag: 'Coastal primary data' },
    { q: 'Which of the following is an example of secondary data for a geographical investigation?', options: ['A questionnaire the researcher carried out themselves', 'Census data collected and published by a national statistics agency', 'A beach profile the researcher measured', 'A pebble count the researcher recorded'], correct: 1, explain: 'Secondary data is data that has already been collected by someone else, such as census data published by a national statistics agency, rather than data collected first-hand by the researcher.', misconception: 'A researcher’s own questionnaire, beach profile or pebble count are all examples of primary data, since they are collected directly by the investigator.', tag: 'Secondary data' },
    { q: 'Historical maps are a useful source of secondary data mainly because they help to:', options: ['Predict future population growth exactly', 'Investigate how a place has changed over time', 'Replace the need for any fieldwork', 'Measure current wave height'], correct: 1, explain: 'Comparing historical maps with current maps or data allows geographers to investigate how a place, such as a coastline or settlement, has changed over time.', misconception: 'Historical maps show change over time, but they cannot precisely predict the future or replace the need for primary fieldwork data.', tag: 'Secondary data' },
    { q: 'A key limitation of using secondary data in an investigation is that it may:', options: ['Always be more accurate than primary data', 'Not have been collected for exactly the same purpose, location or time as the current investigation', 'Never be available online', 'Always match the fieldwork perfectly'], correct: 1, explain: 'A limitation of secondary data is that it was collected by someone else for a different purpose, so it may not exactly match the specific location, time period or focus of the current investigation.', misconception: 'Secondary data isn’t automatically more or less accurate than primary data — its main limitation is that it wasn’t designed specifically for the investigation at hand.', tag: 'Secondary data' },
    { q: 'A divided bar graph is most useful for showing:', options: ['The exact location of data points on a map', 'How a total is split into different proportional categories', 'Correlation between two variables', 'Only single, unrelated values'], correct: 1, explain: 'A divided bar graph shows how a total value is divided into different categories, with each segment’s size representing its proportion of the whole.', misconception: 'A scattergraph, not a divided bar graph, is the technique typically used to show correlation between two variables.', tag: 'Data presentation — graphs' },
    { q: 'A scattergraph is the most appropriate technique for showing:', options: ['The relationship or correlation between two numerical variables', 'Change in one variable over regular time intervals', 'Data split into proportional categories', 'The physical shape of a beach'], correct: 0, explain: 'A scattergraph plots pairs of data points for two variables, making it the most appropriate technique for showing whether, and how strongly, they are correlated.', misconception: 'A line graph is generally better suited to showing change over time, while a scattergraph specifically shows the relationship between two variables.', tag: 'Data presentation — graphs' },
    { q: 'A choropleth map uses different:', options: ['Line thicknesses to show direction of flow', 'Shading or colours to show how a variable’s value varies between areas', 'Circle sizes to show quantities', 'Contour lines to show height'], correct: 1, explain: 'A choropleth map uses different shades or colours to represent how the value of a variable, such as population density, varies between different areas on a map.', misconception: 'Flow lines and proportional circles are different mapping techniques used to show movement or quantity, not choropleth shading.', tag: 'Data presentation — maps' },
    { q: 'A flow line map is most useful for showing:', options: ['The movement or flow of people, goods or traffic between places, using lines of different widths', 'The variation of a value across areas using shading', 'The frequency distribution of continuous data', 'The relationship between two variables'], correct: 0, explain: 'A flow line map uses lines of varying width to represent the volume and direction of movement, such as traffic flow or migration, between different places.', misconception: 'Choropleth maps show variation using shading, while histograms show frequency distributions — both are different techniques from a flow line map.', tag: 'Data presentation — maps' },
    { q: 'When justifying the choice of a graph or mapping technique for a set of fieldwork data, you should mainly consider:', options: ['Which technique looks the most colourful', 'Which technique best suits the type of data being presented, such as categorical, continuous, or spatial data', 'Which technique takes the least amount of time to draw', 'Always using a pie chart regardless of the data'], correct: 1, explain: 'The most appropriate presentation technique depends on the nature of the data itself, for example whether it is categorical, continuous, or has a spatial/locational dimension.', misconception: 'The choice of technique should be driven by what best represents the type and structure of the data, not by how quick or visually appealing a technique is.', tag: 'Data presentation' },
    { q: 'The mean of a data set is calculated by:', options: ['Identifying the middle value once the data is ordered', 'Adding all the values together and dividing by the number of values', 'Identifying the most frequently occurring value', 'Subtracting the lowest value from the highest value'], correct: 1, explain: 'The mean is calculated by adding together all the values in a data set and dividing the total by the number of values.', misconception: 'Identifying the middle value describes the median, and identifying the most frequent value describes the mode — both are different measures from the mean.', tag: 'Statistical skills' },
    { q: 'The median of a data set is:', options: ['The average of all values', 'The middle value once the data set has been placed in order', 'The most frequently occurring value', 'The difference between the highest and lowest values'], correct: 1, explain: 'The median is found by ordering all the values in a data set from lowest to highest and identifying the middle value, or the average of the two middle values if there is an even number of values.', misconception: 'The average of all values describes the mean, not the median — it’s easy to confuse the two measures of central tendency.', tag: 'Statistical skills' },
    { q: 'The range of a data set shows:', options: ['The most common value', 'The middle value', 'How spread out or varied the data is, calculated as the highest value minus the lowest value', 'The average of the data'], correct: 2, explain: 'The range is calculated by subtracting the lowest value from the highest value, and it indicates how spread out or varied a data set is.', misconception: 'The range measures the spread of data, not its central or typical value — that’s what the mean, median and mode describe instead.', tag: 'Statistical skills' },
    { q: 'Spearman’s Rank Correlation is used to test:', options: ['The average value of a single data set', 'The strength and direction of a relationship between two ranked variables', 'Only categorical, non-numerical data', 'The exact cause of an anomaly'], correct: 1, explain: 'Spearman’s Rank Correlation is a statistical test used to measure the strength and direction, positive or negative, of the relationship between two ranked sets of data.', misconception: 'Spearman’s Rank tests the relationship between two variables, not the average or spread of a single data set on its own.', tag: 'Statistical skills' },
    { q: 'A Spearman’s Rank Correlation result of close to +1 would suggest:', options: ['A strong negative correlation', 'No correlation at all', 'A strong positive correlation', 'The data cannot be analysed'], correct: 2, explain: 'Spearman’s Rank results range between -1 and +1; a result close to +1 indicates a strong positive correlation, meaning both variables tend to increase together.', misconception: 'A result close to -1, not +1, would indicate a strong negative correlation, where one variable increases as the other decreases.', tag: 'Statistical skills' },
    { q: 'The Chi-Squared test is mainly used to investigate data that is:', options: ['Purely numerical and continuous', 'Categorical, by comparing observed results with expected results', 'Always perfectly correlated', 'Only available as secondary data'], correct: 1, explain: 'The Chi-Squared test is mainly used with categorical data, comparing the observed results from an investigation with the results that would be expected if there were no significant relationship, to assess a hypothesis.', misconception: 'Chi-Squared is suited to categorical data and comparing observed versus expected frequencies, unlike Spearman’s Rank, which tests correlation between two ranked numerical variables.', tag: 'Statistical skills' },
    { q: 'When two variables show a negative correlation, this means that:', options: ['Both variables increase together', 'As one variable increases, the other tends to decrease', 'There is no relationship between the variables at all', 'The data must contain an error'], correct: 1, explain: 'A negative correlation means that as one variable increases, the other variable tends to decrease — an inverse relationship shown by points sloping downward on a scattergraph.', misconception: 'Both variables increasing together describes a positive correlation, which is the opposite pattern.', tag: 'Analysing results' },
    { q: 'An anomaly (or outlier) in a set of fieldwork results is:', options: ['The most typical, expected result in the data set', 'A result that doesn’t fit the general pattern or trend shown by the rest of the data', 'Always caused by equipment failure', 'Always removed from the data set without explanation'], correct: 1, explain: 'An anomaly, or outlier, is a result that stands out from the general pattern or trend in the rest of the data, and it should be explained with reference to specific evidence rather than ignored.', misconception: 'An anomaly doesn’t have to be caused by equipment error — it might reflect a genuine, unusual local factor, and it should be investigated and explained, not simply discarded.', tag: 'Analysing results' },
    { q: 'Describing spatial variations in fieldwork data means:', options: ['Explaining how a value changes with time of day only', 'Describing how a value changes from place to place across the study area', 'Listing every individual data value with no analysis', 'Ignoring the location of each data point'], correct: 1, explain: 'Describing spatial variation involves explaining how a variable’s value changes from place to place across the sites studied, often supported by specific figures and site references.', misconception: 'Spatial variation is about differences between locations, not changes over time, which would be a temporal pattern instead.', tag: 'Analysing results' },
    { q: 'Hydraulic action erodes the coast mainly by:', options: ['Rocks grinding against each other and becoming smaller', 'The sheer force of waves compressing air in cracks in the cliff, weakening the rock', 'Acidic sea water slowly dissolving certain rock types', 'Waves carrying sand along the coast'], correct: 1, explain: 'Hydraulic action occurs when waves crash against a cliff, compressing air trapped in cracks and joints; the pressure this creates weakens and eventually breaks the rock apart.', misconception: 'Rocks grinding against each other describes attrition, and acidic water dissolving rock describes solution — both are different erosion processes from hydraulic action.', tag: 'Coastal knowledge — processes' },
    { q: 'Attrition is the process by which:', options: ['Sediment particles collide with each other, becoming smaller and more rounded over time', 'Waves compress air in rock cracks', 'Soluble rock is dissolved by sea water', 'Material is carried within the body of the water'], correct: 0, explain: 'Attrition occurs when sediment particles being transported collide with each other, gradually breaking off fragments and becoming smaller and more rounded.', misconception: 'Compressing air in rock cracks describes hydraulic action, and dissolving soluble rock describes solution — both are erosion processes distinct from attrition, which affects transported sediment.', tag: 'Coastal knowledge — processes' },
    { q: 'Saltation is a form of coastal transportation in which:', options: ['Sediment is dissolved and carried invisibly in the water', 'Fine particles are carried within the flow of the water', 'Larger particles are rolled or dragged along the sea bed', 'Particles bounce or hop along the sea bed'], correct: 3, explain: 'Saltation is the bouncing or hopping movement of medium-sized particles along the sea bed, caused by the flow of water.', misconception: 'Rolling or dragging particles along the bed describes traction, and carrying fine particles within the water describes suspension — both are distinct transportation processes from saltation.', tag: 'Coastal knowledge — processes' },
    { q: 'Groynes are a coastal management strategy that work mainly by:', options: ['Dissolving incoming wave energy chemically', 'Trapping sediment moved by longshore drift, building up the beach on one side', 'Allowing the coastline to retreat naturally', 'Adding new sand directly onto the beach'], correct: 1, explain: 'Groynes are wooden or rock structures built out from the beach that trap sediment being moved along the coast by longshore drift, building up the beach and providing a wider buffer against wave energy.', misconception: 'Adding new sand directly to a beach describes beach nourishment, and allowing natural retreat describes managed retreat — both are different strategies from groynes.', tag: 'Coastal knowledge — management' },
    { q: 'Beach nourishment is a soft-engineering strategy that involves:', options: ['Building a rigid concrete wall along the coast', 'Adding sand or shingle to a beach to build it up and absorb wave energy', 'Placing large boulders at the base of a cliff', 'Relocating an entire settlement away from the coast'], correct: 1, explain: 'Beach nourishment, or replenishment, involves adding sand or shingle to a beach, widening and building it up so it can absorb wave energy more effectively and provide a natural buffer.', misconception: 'A concrete wall and placing boulders describe hard-engineering strategies (sea walls and rock armour), while relocation describes managed retreat — all different from beach nourishment.', tag: 'Coastal knowledge — management' }
  ];

